---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Patch Bay – Privacy policy"
  exactTitle="Patch Bay – Privacy policy"
  path="/patchbay/privacy/"
  description="Privacy policy for Patch Bay, a free Claude plugin for 70s and 80s electronic pop. It runs in your own Claude account, and its author sees nothing unless you choose to send it."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Patch Bay</p>
  <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
  <p class="post-meta" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Last updated 3 November 2026</p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      Patch Bay runs inside your own Claude account. I have no servers, no
      tracking and no analytics of my own, and I never see your
      conversations, your playlists or anything you save. The only way
      anything reaches me is if you choose to send it.
    </p>

    <h2>Who I am</h2>
    <p>
      Patch Bay is a free, open-source (MIT) plugin by Laurence Timms, an
      individual developer in the UK. The source is on GitHub. It is a
      synth-nerd companion for 70s and 80s electronic pop: chart weeks with
      playlists, which machines made a record's sound (with honest confidence
      grades), and a game, Six Degrees of Being Boiled.
    </p>

    <h2>What the plugin is</h2>
    <p>
      A set of written instructions, a small data file of records, machines
      and people, and some scripts. Claude follows the instructions and runs
      the scripts inside your own Claude account. No separate software runs
      anywhere else.
    </p>

    <h2>What data is involved, and where it lives</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Web research.</strong> To check charts and credits, Claude
        searches the web and reads public pages, such as the Official Charts
        archive, Sound On Sound and Wikipedia. Searches contain record,
        artist and machine names, not personal details.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The gear graph.</strong> The records, machines and people
        that ship with the plugin come from public sources, each one linked.
        It contains no information about you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Session finds.</strong> Links Claude finds while you play are
        kept in a file in your Claude session. They stay there unless you
        choose to send them to me.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Playlists.</strong> If you've connected a music service to
        Claude, such as the Spotify connector, Patch Bay can create a
        playlist in your account. It asks first. If you haven't connected
        one, you get a plain track list instead.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The live Map of Being Boiled.</strong> If you publish the
        map, it is a page in your own Claude account, private to you until
        you share it. Its Claude features (Ask the map, Tell the story,
        Suggest leads) use the Claude usage of whoever is viewing, and Claude
        asks that person before the first use. If someone saves a lead, the
        page stores the lead, the record it's about, the time, and an
        anonymous Claude account id for the person who saved it. Anyone you
        share the page with can see saved leads. When you ask Patch Bay in
        chat to check the leads, it updates them on the page.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Third parties</h2>
    <p>
      Claude is provided by Anthropic, under <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy">Anthropic's privacy policy</a>.
      That covers your conversations, the published map and its saved leads.
      If you use a music connector, the service behind it, such as Spotify,
      handles your playlists under its own policy. I have no access to any of
      it.
    </p>

    <h2>Usage figures from the directory</h2>
    <!-- Laurence: confirm against the directory portal's Usage tab before publishing. -->
    <p>
      If Patch Bay is listed in Anthropic's plugin directory, I may see
      aggregate figures such as installs, versions in use, how often it runs
      and error rates. I don't see what you asked, what Claude answered, or
      anything you saved.
    </p>

    <h2>Things you send me</h2>
    <p>
      If you send me a finds file or a lead, for example by email or as a
      GitHub issue, I'll receive what you send, along with whatever your
      email or GitHub account shows. I use it only to check and improve the
      gear graph. A confirmed find may be added to the public graph with its
      source, but never your name unless you ask to be credited.
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping and deleting your data</h2>
    <p>
      I keep nothing unless you send it to me. To remove Patch Bay's data,
      delete the published map (which deletes its saved leads), any playlists
      it made, and the plugin itself. Requests about data held by Anthropic or
      a music service go to those companies. If you've sent me something and
      want it removed, use the contact form and I'll delete it.
    </p>

    <h2>This web page</h2>
    <p>
      This page sets no cookies and uses no analytics or tracking. Fonts are
      served by Google Fonts, which receives your IP address and browser
      details. The site is hosted on Cloudflare.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>Patch Bay is not intended for anyone under 18.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, I'll post the new version here with a new "last
      updated" date. Significant changes to what the plugin does are noted in
      its README on GitHub.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about this policy go through the <a href="/coaching/#contact">contact form</a> on my site.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
